<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<head>
  <title>J2KIMG: Conversion between JPEG2000 and IMG or JPEG raster
  formats</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>

<body>
 <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
  <p>The JPEG2000 files are rasters highly compressed
  thanks to the use of transformed wavelet. JPEG2000 uses typically degrading
  compression (it also named unconservative, with loss, or lossy), for being
  the one that more strongly compresses the image; despite this unconserving
  character, the images usually have a visual aspect very similar to the
  original ones, unless they have been generated at compression levels
  extremely raised; this is an important difference with regard to the JPEG
  conventional format, which starts presenting visual gadgetry at minor levels
  of compression. On the other hand, the JPEG format can also compress in an
  efficient way even without degrading the values of the original image
  (conserving compression, without loss, or lossless), even if in this case
  the levels of compression are not so high.</p>

  <p>This application allows you to obtain the information of the internal metadata
  of the JPEG2000 format, to import a JPEG2000 to a JPEG format (grey or RGB,
  depending on the JPEG2000 file) or to extract between one and three IMG
  bands of a JPEG2000.Thereby, depending on your interest the program allows you to execute different options:</p>
  <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.--> 
  <p>When the JPEG2000 images have been compressed without loss, it can have
  sense to recover the images as IMG files to analyse them, although these
  file might need much more disc space than the original images, and if the
  images need more than 2 Gbyte for spectral band it could be impossible to
  convert.</p>
  <hr/>

  <exclusiva>
